 ShellRefinement 6.7.1. A pill's edge is a per-app fact, keyed by focus id,
 defaulting to the band's own edge until something writes one. Damian ruled
 the flick's DIRECTION the selection criterion on 2026-08-27; this is the
 place the direction will be spent, and this stage lands it inert.

 THE INERTNESS IS THE CLAIM AND IT IS THE HALF WORTH ASSERTING. Nothing writes
 an entry yet, so every pill must still answer the band's edge at all four
 edges. A row that only checked "a written value comes back" would pass with
 the default broken, and a row that only checked the default would pass if the
 block were never allocated at all -- so both are here and they fail for
 different reasons.

 ZERO IS THE TRAP. `alloc-zeroed` hands back zeros and `dk-edge-bottom` is 0,
 so an unwritten slot and a slot flicked to the bottom are the same bits unless
 the stored value is offset. The row that discriminates is **writing
 `dk-edge-bottom` explicitly and requiring it to survive a band whose edge is
 something else**: with a raw store that reads back as the band's edge and the
 defect is invisible at the default geometry.

Section: Body

  dpe-yn : Boolean -> Text
  dpe-yn (v) = if v then "yes" else "NO"

  dpe-defaults : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Text
  dpe-defaults (ds) (e) (i) =
    if i >= dk-pedge-slots then "all default to the band"
    else if dk-pedge-get ds i /= e then "id " & integer-to-text i & " answered " & integer-to-text (dk-pedge-get ds i) & " not " & integer-to-text e
    else dpe-defaults ds e (i + 1)

  dpe-row : Integer, Integer -> Text
  dpe-row (ds) (e) =
    let sp = poke-32 ds dk-task-edge-cell e
    in "edge " & dk-edge-name e
       & "  band " & integer-to-text (dk-task-edge ds)
       & "  unwritten id 3 " & integer-to-text (dk-pedge-get ds 3)
       & "  default follows band " & dpe-yn (dk-pedge-get ds 3 == e)
       & "  " & dpe-defaults ds e 0

 A WRITE MUST SURVIVE, AND THE ONE THAT DISCRIMINATES IS `dk-edge-bottom`
 WRITTEN UNDER A BAND THAT IS NOT AT THE BOTTOM. The other three edges would
 read back correctly even from a raw store.

  dpe-write : Integer -> Text
  dpe-write (ds) =
    let sp = poke-32 ds dk-task-edge-cell dk-edge-top
    in let a = dk-pedge-set ds 5 dk-edge-bottom
    in let b = dk-pedge-set ds 6 dk-edge-left
    in "band top, wrote bottom to id 5 and left to id 6"
       & "  id 5 " & integer-to-text (dk-pedge-get ds 5)
       & "  survives " & dpe-yn (dk-pedge-get ds 5 == dk-edge-bottom)
       & "  id 6 " & integer-to-text (dk-pedge-get ds 6)
       & "  survives " & dpe-yn (dk-pedge-get ds 6 == dk-edge-left)
       & "  untouched id 7 still band " & dpe-yn (dk-pedge-get ds 7 == dk-edge-top)

 OUT OF RANGE ANSWERS THE BAND RATHER THAN READING PAST THE BLOCK. The block
 is `dk-pedge-slots` entries and `dk-focus-max` is 16, so id 16 is the last
 valid one and 17 is the first that must be refused.

  dpe-bounds : Integer -> Text
  dpe-bounds (ds) =
    let sp = poke-32 ds dk-task-edge-cell dk-edge-right
    in "out of range: id -1 " & integer-to-text (dk-pedge-get ds (0 - 1))
       & "  id 17 " & integer-to-text (dk-pedge-get ds 17)
       & "  both answer the band " & dpe-yn (dk-pedge-get ds (0 - 1) == dk-edge-right & dk-pedge-get ds 17 == dk-edge-right)

 6.7.2. THE BAND FILTERS BY EDGE, AND THE CLAIM THAT CAN FAIL IS THAT A PILL
 WRITTEN ELSEWHERE LEAVES THE BAND'S SET. The inert half -- everything default,
 so the band still yields every open window -- would pass with the filter
 deleted, because `dk-pedge-get` answers the band's edge for every app until
 something writes one. Only the second half distinguishes a filter that works
 from no filter at all.

 The registry is built by hand rather than by opening windows: `dk-wr-find`
 reads a count at offset 0 and a focus id at `4 * i + 4`, which is the whole
 of what `dk-pills` asks of it.

 IT USES IDS 8, 9 AND 10 BECAUSE THE ROWS ABOVE WROTE 5 AND 6, and a `ds` is
 shared down the whole entry. Built on 5 and 6 this row read "band had 2" of
 three registered windows -- self-consistent, and quietly depending on a
 previous row's side effect. A row whose baseline is another row's leftovers
 is a row that changes meaning when anything above it is edited.

  dpe-ws : Integer
  dpe-ws =
    let ws = alloc-zeroed 64 64
    in let n = poke-32 ws 0 3
    in let a = poke-32 ws 4 8
    in let b = poke-32 ws 8 9
    in let c = poke-32 ws 12 10
    in ws

  dpe-count : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dpe-count (ws) (ds) (e) = list-length (dk-pills ws ds e 0 [])

  dpe-filter : Integer -> Text
  dpe-filter (ds) =
    let ws = dpe-ws
    in let sp = poke-32 ds dk-task-edge-cell dk-edge-bottom
    in let before = dpe-count ws ds dk-edge-bottom
    in let w = dk-pedge-set ds 9 dk-edge-right
    in let after = dpe-count ws ds dk-edge-bottom
    in let moved = dpe-count ws ds dk-edge-right
    in "filter: band had " & integer-to-text before
       & "  after moving id 9 to the right the band has " & integer-to-text after
       & "  and the right edge has " & integer-to-text moved
       & "  band lost exactly one " & dpe-yn (after == before - 1)
       & "  the right gained it " & dpe-yn (moved == 1)
       & "  nothing vanished " & dpe-yn (after + moved == before)

 6.7.3a. THE CONTENT BOX ASKS EVERY EDGE, and the two claims fail for
 different reasons. **Inert**: with nothing written, the box is exactly what
 the old band-keyed arithmetic gave at all four edges, so this stage moves no
 picture. **Live**: writing a pill to an edge that is not the band's must
 SHRINK the box on that edge, which is what would fail if `dk-cbox-*` were
 still keyed on `dk-task-edge` alone.

 The inert half is checked against the arithmetic the old code did rather than
 against `dk-strip-px` itself, or it would be comparing the new function with
 itself (L-BOTHARMS).

 ONE OF THE SHRINK ROW'S THREE VERDICTS IS VACUOUS AGAINST THE OBVIOUS BREAK
 AND IT IS KEPT AND SAID SO. Reverting `dk-cbox-w` to the band-keyed form moves
 "took glass" and "took the measured strip" to NO and leaves "gave it back"
 at yes, because with nothing ever taken the width trivially returns to where
 it started. It stays because it fails against a DIFFERENT defect -- a strip
 that reserves and never releases -- and a reader who saw only the two moving
 verdicts would not know which break each one covers.

  dpe-oldbox : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Text
  dpe-oldbox (ds) (w) (h) (e) =
    let sp = poke-32 ds dk-task-edge-cell e
    in let ox = if e == dk-edge-left then dk-task-px ds w else 0
    in let oy = dk-bar-h * ui-scale w + (if e == dk-edge-top then dk-task-px ds w else 0)
    in let ow = w - ox - (if e == dk-edge-right then dk-task-px ds w else 0)
    in let oh = h - oy - (if e == dk-edge-bottom then dk-task-px ds w else 0)
    in "box " & dk-edge-name e
       & "  x " & integer-to-text (dk-cbox-x w ds) & "/" & integer-to-text ox
       & "  y " & integer-to-text (dk-cbox-y w ds) & "/" & integer-to-text oy
       & "  w " & integer-to-text (dk-cbox-w w ds) & "/" & integer-to-text ow
       & "  h " & integer-to-text (dk-cbox-h w h ds) & "/" & integer-to-text oh
       & "  unchanged " & dpe-yn (dk-cbox-x w ds == ox & dk-cbox-y w ds == oy & dk-cbox-w w ds == ow & dk-cbox-h w h ds == oh)

 A REGISTERED WINDOW IS REQUIRED, not just a written edge: `dk-strip-any` asks
 the registry first, so a pill for an app with no open window reserves nothing.
 That is the arm for "the strip disappears when the edge has none".

 THIS ROW BUILDS ITS OWN `ds` AND THAT IS NOT TIDINESS. Written against the
 shared one it read a baseline width of 1360 rather than 1600, because
 `dpe-filter` above had already docked id 9 to the right and id 9 is in the
 registry -- so the right edge was already carrying a strip before the row
 began, writing another pill there changed nothing, and "the right took glass"
 answered NO against working code. **The chapter had already been bitten by
 this once and the repair then was to move the fixture's ids**, which fixed the
 symptom one row wide and left the shape intact. A row that asserts a
 DIFFERENCE has to own every input the difference depends on.

  dpe-shrink : Integer, Integer -> Text
  dpe-shrink (w) (h) =
    let ds = alloc-zeroed 256 64
    in let ap = poke-32 ds dk-adorn-cell dk-adorn-default
    in let pei = dk-pedge-init ds
    in let ws = dpe-ws
    in let wr = poke-32 ds dk-wr-cell ws
    in let sp = poke-32 ds dk-task-edge-cell dk-edge-bottom
    in let w0 = dk-cbox-w w ds
    in let m = dk-pedge-set ds 8 dk-edge-right
    in let w1 = dk-cbox-w w ds
    in let u = dk-pedge-set ds 8 dk-edge-bottom
    in let w2 = dk-cbox-w w ds
    in "shrink: width " & integer-to-text w0
       & "  with a pill on the right " & integer-to-text w1
       & "  moved back " & integer-to-text w2
       & "  the right took glass " & dpe-yn (w1 < w0)
       & "  and gave it back " & dpe-yn (w2 == w0)
       & "  it took the measured strip " & dpe-yn (w0 - w1 == dk-strip-depth ds w dk-edge-right)

 6.7.3b. THE STRIP PAINTS, and the claim is that the pill is LAID on its own
 edge rather than merely accounted for. The reservation rows above prove the
 content box shrinks; they would all still pass if the strip were never put in
 the tree and the glass simply went missing.

 THE ASSERTION IS THE PILL'S RECTANGLE AGAINST THE CONTENT BOX, not against
 the strip's own rectangle, which would be the new code agreeing with itself
 (L-BOTHARMS). A pill docked right must lie entirely beyond the box's right
 edge; the same pill on the band's edge must not.

 IT CALLS `dk-task-init` AND `dk-strip-init` BECAUSE `desk-run` DOES. Without
 them the band and strip cells are unwritten and `dk-strip-depth` answers its
 FALLBACK floor rather than the measured extent -- 240 device pixels against
 the measured 280 at 1600 -- so the row laid a strip 140 logical wide into a
 reservation of 120 and read the pill 2 pixels inside the box. That is an arm
 measuring a configuration the desk never runs, not a defect in the desk, and
 it is the same shape as measuring a face at a ppem the desk would not load.

  dpe-pill-x : WidgetNode, Text -> Integer
  dpe-pill-x (laid) (id) =
    when widget-find laid id
      is Just (n) -> n.wn-bounds.lr-x
      is None -> 0 - 1

  dpe-paint : Integer, Integer -> Text
  dpe-paint (w) (h) =
    let ds = alloc-zeroed 256 64
    in let ap = poke-32 ds dk-adorn-cell dk-adorn-default
    in let pei = dk-pedge-init ds
    in let wr = poke-32 ds dk-wr-cell dpe-ws
    in let sp = poke-32 ds dk-task-edge-cell dk-edge-bottom
    in let tki = dk-task-init ds gfont-none w
    in let sti = dk-strip-init ds gfont-none w
    in let s = ui-wscale w
    in let onband = dpe-pill-x (widget-layout (desk-chrome-with (widget-panel "content" DirColumn 0 []) w ds) (dk-theme-ds ds) (layout-rect 0 0 (w / s) (h / s))) (dk-pill-id 8)
    in let mv = dk-pedge-set ds 8 dk-edge-right
    in let onright = dpe-pill-x (widget-layout (desk-chrome-with (widget-panel "content" DirColumn 0 []) w ds) (dk-theme-ds ds) (layout-rect 0 0 (w / s) (h / s))) (dk-pill-id 8)
    in let boxr = (dk-cbox-x w ds + dk-cbox-w w ds) / s
    in "paint: pill 8 on the band at x " & integer-to-text onband
       & "  docked right at x " & integer-to-text onright
       & "  box right edge " & integer-to-text boxr
       & "  found in both " & dpe-yn (onband >= 0 & onright >= 0)
       & "  it moved " & dpe-yn (onright /= onband)
       & "  and it is beyond the box " & dpe-yn (onright >= boxr)

 6.7.4. THE FLICK SPENDS THE DIRECTION. The gesture above this is a speed and
 a deadline read off the HPET, and `desk.ps1 -Rtc` pins the HPET, so a flick
 cannot be driven by a scripted capture at all. What this stage adds is not a
 reading but what happens once one is taken, and `dk-flick-dock-at` is that
 step on its own so an arm can drive it.

 EACH DIRECTION IS ASKED SEPARATELY BECAUSE ONE WOULD NOT DISCRIMINATE. A
 dock that ignored its argument and always wrote the band's edge passes any
 single-direction row taken at the band's own edge; the four together cannot
 be satisfied by a constant. The band sits at the BOTTOM throughout, so the
 bottom row is also the control that a real write and a defaulted read are
 told apart.

  dpe-dock : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Text
  dpe-dock (ds) (fid) (dir) =
    let d = dk-flick-dock-at ds fid dir
    in " " & dk-edge-name dir & "=" & dk-edge-name (dk-pedge-get ds fid)
       & dpe-yn (dk-pedge-get ds fid == dir)

  dpe-flick : Integer -> Text
  dpe-flick (w) =
    let ds = alloc-zeroed 256 64
    in let ap = poke-32 ds dk-adorn-cell dk-adorn-default
    in let pei = dk-pedge-init ds
    in let wr = poke-32 ds dk-wr-cell dpe-ws
    in let sp = poke-32 ds dk-task-edge-cell dk-edge-bottom
    in "flick docks where it points, band at the bottom:"
       & dpe-dock ds 8 dk-edge-left
       & dpe-dock ds 9 dk-edge-right
       & dpe-dock ds 10 dk-edge-top
       & dpe-dock ds 8 dk-edge-bottom
       & "  and the window minimised " & dpe-yn (peek-32 (desk-wnd-blk 8 ds) dk-wnd-st-cell == dk-wnd-st-min)

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing =
    let ds = alloc-zeroed 256 64
    in let ap = poke-32 ds dk-adorn-cell dk-adorn-default
    in let pei = dk-pedge-init ds
    in act
      print-line-uni (dpe-row ds dk-edge-bottom)
      print-line-uni (dpe-row ds dk-edge-top)
      print-line-uni (dpe-row ds dk-edge-left)
      print-line-uni (dpe-row ds dk-edge-right)
      print-line-uni (dpe-write ds)
      print-line-uni (dpe-bounds ds)
      print-line-uni (dpe-filter ds)
      print-line-uni (dpe-oldbox ds 1600 900 dk-edge-bottom)
      print-line-uni (dpe-oldbox ds 1600 900 dk-edge-top)
      print-line-uni (dpe-oldbox ds 1600 900 dk-edge-left)
      print-line-uni (dpe-oldbox ds 1600 900 dk-edge-right)
      print-line-uni (dpe-shrink 1600 900)
      print-line-uni (dpe-paint 1600 900)
      print-line-uni (dpe-flick 1600)
    end

